FILE: commit_msg_571.txt
#571 ppcdsformxo: the PowerPC decoder folded the DS-form XO bits into the displacement, so ldu/lwa ran as ld at EA+1/EA+2 and stdu ran as std at EA+1 with no update -- opcodes 58 and 62 now decode their five forms, reserved encodings and RA=0 update forms stop the cpu, a 39-row rung-3 detector in gate 13

src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c (+31/-2, two hunks): in X(to_be_translated)'s D/DS-form arm the `case PPC_HI6_LD:` and
`case PPC_HI6_STD:` bodies of the inner switch re-assign the shared imm to (int16_t)(iword & ~3) -- EXTS(DS || 0b00),
so the table index (imm == 0) and ic->arg[2] both see the masked displacement -- and dispatch on iword & 3: 58 -> ld
(XO 0), ldu (XO 1, update), lwa (XO 2, size 2 and zero 0: the existing sign-extending word handler, slot 10 of the
generated table), XO 3 -> the arm's bare goto bad; 62 -> std (XO 0), stdu (XO 1, update), XO 2 and 3 -> goto bad.  No
handler changes (every form selects a slot tmp_ppc_loadstore.c already holds; none reaches ppc_instr_invalid).  Two
behaviour changes besides the displacement: a reserved XO, which executed as ld/std at EA+2/EA+3, now stops the cpu
through the common tail (UNIMPLEMENTED instruction, printed under single-step too; nothing is delivered to the guest --
the tree has no Program interrupt); ldu/stdu with RA = 0, which executed at the absolute address EXTS(DS || XO) with no
update, now reach the arm's existing ra == 0 && update guard.  ldu with RA = RT (an invalid form) is disclosed, not
checked, as the sibling update loads do; stdu with RA = RS is valid and stores the old RS.  32-bit CPUs: opcodes 58/62
are illegal there (PEM :6253); before and after this fix a 32-bit cpu decodes them through the same arm (one source builds
both decoders; the MODE32 table holds real doubleword handlers); no width test this round -- filed as ppc64on32gate.
The shared `imm = (int16_t)iword;` and the other 22 opcodes of the arm are untouched.  Byte-identical in est/.

